From 7a5465bc6f2c263b1c980b8bb78486a670d806ba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Romeu Melur <romeumello@icloud.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 14:53:43 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] virtio/vsock/unix_proxy: don't block on a host peer that
 stops reading

Once connected, the unix proxy switched the host socket to blocking mode
and wrote guest data with send(). The TX queue is processed on the thread
that also serves every other vsock connection and the other devices, so a
host peer that stopped reading blocked that thread on the full socket.
On macOS this froze the whole guest: the virtio console waits for the
host with interrupts masked, so a CPU writing to it stalled, and RCU
reported stalls. A pipeline such as `docker export | docker run -i`
through the proxy hung that way for an hour.

Keep the socket non-blocking. Data the socket doesn't take waits in a
per-connection buffer and the proxy polls for output; the credit given
back to the guest only advances as bytes reach the host, so the guest
stops sending and the buffer stays below the buffer size the proxy
advertises. A shutdown of the guest's send side, or of both sides, waits
until the buffer is written.

Add a vsock-unix-slow-reader end-to-end test: the guest streams 16 MiB on
one connection that the host leaves unread, the host expects an answer
on a second connection meanwhile, then reads and checks the whole
stream. Without this change it times out.

Assisted-by: Claude Code: claude-opus-5.5
Signed-off-by: Romeu Melur <romeumello@icloud.com>
